From 746d6ea049bc58ca785798220f86101ed8cbfcc3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 06:50:14 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] EDEN_UP_ARBITER_SPIN_US=<1 to 20>: an address wait watches
 the word for that many microseconds before it sleeps (javocsoft/Lichii
 e0ae6b0, from Lichii #717); off by default

Ported from javocsoft/Lichii e0ae6b08b9 ("kernel, memory: cheaper locks, waits and GPU memory
traps", JavocSoft), which takes it from zortos293/Lichii PR 717 (open draft, eb3553bcb8) by
stephenjayakar, where it is EDEN_NX2_ARB_SPIN and on at 8 us. Credit to both. Only the address
arbiter spin is taken; the rest of that commit is not in this patch (see below).

Why: a tester's PS5 profile had about 39% of Ocarina's main thread in WaitForAddress on its own
job workers. Every such wait that sleeps costs the scheduler lock, a context switch, then the
signaler's EndWait, an interrupt of the waiter's core, a host wake (umtx on the PS5) and the
switch back. When the worker signals a few microseconds after the main thread started to wait,
all of that is overhead on the main thread's critical path, and the signaler's SignalToAddress
gets cheaper too (no waiter in the tree, so no EndWait and no interrupt).

What it does, with EDEN_UP_ARBITER_SPIN_US=N (1 to 20; above 20 reads as 20):
- WaitIfLessThan and WaitIfEqual (32 and 64 bit), with a non-zero timeout, first watch the word
  without any lock for up to N us, with a pause between reads.
- If the word stops satisfying the wait, the SVC returns ResultInvalidState, which is what the
  kernel returns to a thread that entered the SVC that much later. The guest already handles it
  (it re-checks the word itself).
- The spin stops early when the core is interrupted (another thread must run there) and in
  single-core mode or on a misaligned address it is not tried.
- Per thread credit (0-16, starts at 8): +2 for a spin that paid off, -1 for one that ran out. At
  0 the thread spins on one wait in 16 only. A thread whose waits are long stops wasting time.
- A terminating thread takes the normal path, which reports the termination first.
- Log (Kernel, Info): one line at the first wait with the setting, then every 16,384 spins:
  spins, how many ended without a sleep (%), how many slept after the full spin, how many were cut
  short by the core, how many were skipped by the credit, and the mean pauses of a paying and of a
  full spin (their ratio times N is the mean time a paying spin took).
Off (unset, empty, 0 or not plain digits): SpinBeforeWait returns at once and every wait runs
exactly as before. Layout only: two u8 fields at the end of KThread.

Deviations from upstream: the switch name and off by default (upstream: on at 8 us); the time and
interrupt checks run every 16 pauses instead of 32, so a short spin ends closer to its time; the
time comes from Common::g_wall_clock (the TSC, as the front's NowNs) instead of steady_clock,
because clock_gettime is a system call of about 890 ns on the console and would put most of a
2 us spin in the host kernel; the counters and log lines are ours. The value is read once with getenv and taken only as plain
digits: a number cannot go through Common::PeSwitch, which takes exactly "1" (as for
EDEN_UP_OOT_POLL_BACKOFF_US). EDEN_UP_ARBITER_SPIN_US=1 is a 1 us spin.

What to expect on the PS5. The front's PhysicalCore::Idle (headless/CMakeLists.txt) already
spins on the interrupt flag before it sleeps (idle_spin_us=, default 5,000 pauses, about 100 us),
so a guest core that goes idle mostly avoids the host wake already. What this adds is skipping the
guest kernel's own sleep and wake: two scheduler-lock passes, the arbiter tree, BeginWait/EndWait,
the interrupt and two fiber switches, for every handoff that comes within N. So expect a smaller
gain than upstream's desktop number (which also had the other changes listed below), and only if
the game's handoffs are frequent and short; the log line says how many are.

It can also cost. Guest cores 4 and 5 are 82-88% busy and the guest has more ready threads than
cores. A spinning wait holds its guest core, and a lower priority thread that is ready there (one
that would run the moment this thread slept) waits out the spin, since only a scheduling change
interrupts it. On a core that would go idle instead, the spin burns nothing the idle spin would
not. Worst steady case: about one wait in three pays off, so the credit stays up and two thirds of
the waits burn the whole N, near N * 2/3 us per wait: at 2 us and, say, 5,000 waits a second on a
thread, under 1% of a core; at 8 us, close to 3%. So start with 2, then 4; 8 (upstream) only if
4 helps.

Not taken from e0ae6b0: KSpinLock's 20 us budget (ours spins 128 pauses before it sleeps), the
PhysicalCore spin lock and interrupt-flag idle, KThread's atomic context guard, constinit thread
locals, GetParentReference offsets, SpinRecursiveMutex::try_lock and the GPU memory trap changes.
---
 src/core/hle/kernel/k_address_arbiter.cpp | 163 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 src/core/hle/kernel/k_thread.h            |   6 +
 2 files changed, 169 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/core/hle/kernel/k_address_arbiter.cpp b/src/core/hle/kernel/k_address_arbiter.cpp
index 3415016..42781ad 100644
--- a/src/core/hle/kernel/k_address_arbiter.cpp
+++ b/src/core/hle/kernel/k_address_arbiter.cpp
@@ -4,6 +4,17 @@
 // SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright 2021 yuzu Emulator Project
 // SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
 
+#include <algorithm>
+#include <atomic>
+#include <chrono>
+#include <cstdlib>
+
+#if defined(_M_X64) || defined(_M_IX86) || defined(__x86_64__) || defined(__i386__)
+#include <immintrin.h>
+#endif
+
+#include "common/cpu_features.h"
+#include "common/logging.h"
 #include "core/arm/exclusive_monitor.h"
 #include "core/core.h"
 #include "core/hle/kernel/k_address_arbiter.h"
@@ -14,6 +25,7 @@
 #include "core/hle/kernel/k_thread_queue.h"
 #include "core/hle/kernel/k_typed_address.h"
 #include "core/hle/kernel/kernel.h"
+#include "core/hle/kernel/physical_core.h"
 #include "core/hle/kernel/svc_results.h"
 #include "core/memory.h"
 
@@ -111,6 +123,147 @@ bool UpdateIfEqual(KernelCore& kernel, s32* out, KProcessAddress address, s32 va
     return true;
 }
 
+/// EDEN_UP_ARBITER_SPIN_US=<1 to 20>: microseconds a thread about to sleep in WaitIfLessThan or
+/// WaitIfEqual first watches the word (javocsoft/Lichii e0ae6b0, from zortos293/Lichii PR 717 by
+/// stephenjayakar, where it is EDEN_NX2_ARB_SPIN and on at 8). Unset, empty, 0 or anything but
+/// plain digits: off, and every wait takes the path it always did. Above 20 is read as 20.
+constexpr u32 MaxArbiterSpinMicroseconds = 20;
+
+u32 ArbiterSpinMicroseconds() {
+    static const u32 microseconds = [] {
+        const char* const text = std::getenv("EDEN_UP_ARBITER_SPIN_US");
+        if (text == nullptr || text[0] == '\0') {
+            return 0U;
+        }
+        u32 value = 0;
+        for (const char* c = text; *c != '\0'; ++c) {
+            if (*c < '0' || *c > '9') {
+                return 0U;
+            }
+            value = (std::min)(value * 10 + static_cast<u32>(*c - '0'), 1000U);
+        }
+        const u32 result = (std::min)(value, MaxArbiterSpinMicroseconds);
+        if (result != 0) {
+            LOG_INFO(Kernel, "EDEN_UP_ARBITER_SPIN_US: address waits watch the word for {} us "
+                             "before they sleep",
+                     result);
+        }
+        return result;
+    }();
+    return microseconds;
+}
+
+/// Spin counters, logged every ArbiterSpinStatsEvery spins (Kernel, Info), totals since boot.
+struct ArbiterSpinStats {
+    std::atomic<u64> spins{};
+    std::atomic<u64> hits{};        ///< the word changed in time: no sleep, no wake
+    std::atomic<u64> cut{};         ///< stopped early: the core has something else to run
+    std::atomic<u64> skipped{};     ///< the thread's spins kept failing: not tried this time
+    std::atomic<u64> hit_pauses{};  ///< pauses spent in spins that paid off
+    std::atomic<u64> miss_pauses{}; ///< pauses spent in spins that ran out (then slept anyway)
+};
+ArbiterSpinStats g_arbiter_spin_stats;
+constexpr u64 ArbiterSpinStatsEvery = 1ULL << 14;
+
+enum class ArbiterSpinEnd : u8 { Hit, Cut, Miss };
+
+void CountArbiterSpin(u32 spin_us, ArbiterSpinEnd end, u32 pauses) {
+    ArbiterSpinStats& stats = g_arbiter_spin_stats;
+    constexpr auto relaxed = std::memory_order_relaxed;
+    switch (end) {
+    case ArbiterSpinEnd::Hit:
+        stats.hits.fetch_add(1, relaxed);
+        stats.hit_pauses.fetch_add(pauses, relaxed);
+        break;
+    case ArbiterSpinEnd::Cut:
+        stats.cut.fetch_add(1, relaxed);
+        break;
+    case ArbiterSpinEnd::Miss:
+        stats.miss_pauses.fetch_add(pauses, relaxed);
+        break;
+    }
+    const u64 spins = stats.spins.fetch_add(1, relaxed) + 1;
+    if ((spins % ArbiterSpinStatsEvery) != 0) {
+        return;
+    }
+    const u64 hits = stats.hits.load(relaxed);
+    const u64 cut = stats.cut.load(relaxed);
+    const u64 misses = spins > hits + cut ? spins - hits - cut : 0;
+    LOG_INFO(Kernel,
+             "Arbiter spin ({} us): {} spins, {} ended without a sleep ({}%), {} slept after the "
+             "full spin, {} cut short by the core, {} skipped (thread's spins kept failing); "
+             "pauses per paying spin {}, per full spin {}",
+             spin_us, spins, hits, hits * 100 / spins, misses, cut, stats.skipped.load(relaxed),
+             hits != 0 ? stats.hit_pauses.load(relaxed) / hits : 0,
+             misses != 0 ? stats.miss_pauses.load(relaxed) / misses : 0);
+}
+
+/// Guest job systems hand work between threads through these waits many times a frame, and the
+/// signal often comes a few microseconds later. Before the thread sleeps (scheduler lock, context
+/// switch, then the signaler's EndWait, interrupt, host wake and switch back), it watches the word
+/// briefly without any lock. If the word stops satisfying the wait, the wait ends with
+/// ResultInvalidState, exactly what the kernel returns to a thread that entered the SVC that much
+/// later. Threads whose spins rarely pay off stop spinning (m_nx2_arb_spin_credit) and try again
+/// on one wait in 16. Returns true when the wait should end with ResultInvalidState.
+template <typename StillWaits>
+bool SpinBeforeWait(KernelCore& kernel, KThread* cur_thread, uint64_t addr, bool is_64_bit,
+                    StillWaits&& still_waits) {
+    const u32 spin_us = ArbiterSpinMicroseconds();
+    if (spin_us == 0) {
+        return false;
+    }
+    if (!kernel.IsMulticore() || (addr & (is_64_bit ? 7 : 3)) != 0) {
+        return false;
+    }
+    u8& credit = cur_thread->m_nx2_arb_spin_credit;
+    if (credit == 0 && (++cur_thread->m_nx2_arb_spin_probe & 15) != 0) {
+        g_arbiter_spin_stats.skipped.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
+        return false;
+    }
+    const u8* const pointer = GetCurrentMemory(kernel).GetPointerSilent(addr);
+    if (pointer == nullptr) {
+        return false;
+    }
+    const auto load_word = [&]() -> s64 {
+        if (is_64_bit) {
+            return *reinterpret_cast<const volatile s64*>(pointer);
+        }
+        return *reinterpret_cast<const volatile s32*>(pointer);
+    };
+    const PhysicalCore& core = kernel.CurrentPhysicalCore();
+    // The TSC wall clock, not steady_clock: on the console clock_gettime is a system call of
+    // about 890 ns, which would put most of a short spin in the host kernel.
+    const auto deadline = Common::g_wall_clock.GetTimeNS() + std::chrono::microseconds{spin_us};
+    u32 spin = 1;
+    for (;; ++spin) {
+#if defined(_M_X64) || defined(_M_IX86) || defined(__x86_64__) || defined(__i386__)
+        _mm_pause();
+#endif
+        if (!still_waits(load_word())) {
+            credit = static_cast<u8>((std::min)(credit + 2, 16));
+            CountArbiterSpin(spin_us, ArbiterSpinEnd::Hit, spin);
+            // A terminating thread takes the normal path, which reports the termination first.
+            return !cur_thread->IsTerminationRequested();
+        }
+        // Every 16 pauses rather than upstream's 32, so a short spin ends close to its time.
+        if ((spin & 15) == 0) {
+            // Something else must run on this core: stop watching.
+            if (core.IsInterrupted()) {
+                CountArbiterSpin(spin_us, ArbiterSpinEnd::Cut, spin);
+                return false;
+            }
+            if (Common::g_wall_clock.GetTimeNS() >= deadline) {
+                break;
+            }
+        }
+    }
+    if (credit > 0) {
+        --credit;
+    }
+    CountArbiterSpin(spin_us, ArbiterSpinEnd::Miss, spin);
+    return false;
+}
+
 class ThreadQueueImplForKAddressArbiter final : public KThreadQueue {
 public:
     explicit ThreadQueueImplForKAddressArbiter(KernelCore& kernel, KAddressArbiter::ThreadTree* t)
@@ -251,6 +404,11 @@ Result KAddressArbiter::SignalAndModifyByWaitingCountIfEqual(uint64_t addr, s32
 Result KAddressArbiter::WaitIfLessThan(uint64_t addr, s32 value, bool decrement, s64 timeout) {
     // Prepare to wait.
     KThread* cur_thread = GetCurrentThreadPointer(system.Kernel());
+    // EDEN_UP_ARBITER_SPIN_US: watch the word briefly first (off: returns false at once).
+    if (timeout != 0 && SpinBeforeWait(system.Kernel(), cur_thread, addr, false,
+                                       [value](s64 user_value) { return user_value < value; })) {
+        R_THROW(ResultInvalidState);
+    }
     KHardwareTimer* timer{};
     ThreadQueueImplForKAddressArbiter wait_queue(system.Kernel(), std::addressof(m_tree));
 
@@ -306,6 +464,11 @@ Result KAddressArbiter::WaitIfLessThan(uint64_t addr, s32 value, bool decrement,
 Result KAddressArbiter::WaitIfEqual(uint64_t addr, s64 value, s64 timeout, bool is_64_bit) {
     // Prepare to wait.
     KThread* cur_thread = GetCurrentThreadPointer(system.Kernel());
+    // EDEN_UP_ARBITER_SPIN_US: watch the word briefly first (off: returns false at once).
+    if (timeout != 0 && SpinBeforeWait(system.Kernel(), cur_thread, addr, is_64_bit,
+                                       [value](s64 user_value) { return user_value == value; })) {
+        R_THROW(ResultInvalidState);
+    }
     KHardwareTimer* timer{};
     ThreadQueueImplForKAddressArbiter wait_queue(system.Kernel(), std::addressof(m_tree));
 
diff --git a/src/core/hle/kernel/k_thread.h b/src/core/hle/kernel/k_thread.h
index d5fa660..3d49e14 100644
--- a/src/core/hle/kernel/k_thread.h
+++ b/src/core/hle/kernel/k_thread.h
@@ -984,6 +984,12 @@ public:
     ConditionVariableThreadTree* GetConditionVariableTree() const {
         return m_condvar_tree;
     }
+
+    /// KAddressArbiter, EDEN_UP_ARBITER_SPIN_US only: how well this thread's pre-wait spins paid
+    /// off lately (0-16), and a counter that probes again on one wait in 16 while it is 0.
+    /// Touched only by the thread itself, inside its own wait SVC.
+    u8 m_nx2_arb_spin_credit{8};
+    u8 m_nx2_arb_spin_probe{};
 };
 
 class KScopedDisableDispatch {
